  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 322 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Early morning GOES-19 Night Fog product is indicating \nareas of patchy dense valley fog in climo favored areas as temps \nhave dropped into the mid 30s SLK to lower 50s in the CPV. Coolest \nnight of the fall season acrs most of our cwa under 1020mb high pres \ncentered over central NY attm. Sfc high pres wl shift off the New \nEngland Coast this morning with developing southerly flow at all \nlvls expected to develop by this aftn. GOES-19 mid lvl water vapor \nimagery shows potent s/w energy, deep moisture, and associated \nconvection over the Ohio Valley which is progged to angle toward our \ncwa tonight into Sunday. Moisture advection wl increase quickly on \ndeep south to southwest flow with pw values surging btwn 1.50 and \n1.75 by 12z Sunday. In addition, CAM show developing elevated \ninstability parameters under developing waa tonight acrs our central \nand southern cwa. 00z HREF mean qpf btwn 06z-12z shows 0.25 to 0.50\" \nacrs central/southern VT, while only a few hundredths to 0.20\" near \nthe International Border. Based on this and a shift northward in \nboth CAMS and global guidance, have increased qpf and pops, \nespecially acrs central and southern VT zones and portions of the \nHigh Peaks tonight. Temps warm into the 70s today and only cool into \nthe mid 50s to mid 60s tonight with developing clouds/winds and \nprecip anticipated with some increase in humidity levels by 12z \nSunday.\n\nFor Sunday, SPC has expanded the marginal risk for severe to the \nInternational Border, which wl be conditional on how much \nclearing/instability can develop ahead of approaching s/w and cold \nfrnt. Deep layer shear is favorable with values in the 40 to 55 \nknots range, but instability is questionable based on how much sfc \nheating can occur. Thinking strong mid lvl wind field may shear \nconvective updrafts and limit vertical growth, especially with sfc \nbased CAPE values only in the 800 to 1200 J/kg range. If more sfc \nheating/instability can develop the potential for strong to severe \nthunderstorms would increase acrs our cwa on Sunday aftn/evening. \nAdditional showers and embedded thunderstorms are expected btwn 00z-\n09z Monday as potent northern s/w energy drops toward the SLV. \nBetter instability is shifting into eastern New England, so \npotential for svr aft 00z is limited. Temps warm into the mid 70s to \nlower 80s on Sunday and cool back into the lower 40s to lower 50s by \nMonday morning. Based on cyclonic northwest flow and associated caa, \nexpect clouds to linger, especially acrs the upslope areas of the \nnorthern Dacks and central/northern Greens on Monday morning, before \nhigh pres clears the skies by afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A progged 1026mb high pres builds directly overhead by \n00z Tuesday with mostly clear skies and light winds anticipated \novernight. This wl allow for temps to quickly fall back into the \nupper 20s SLK to mid/upper 40s CPV by 12z Tuesday with areas of \npatchy frost and fog likely. Our fast flow aloft prevails for Tues \nthru most of the upcoming week with increasing chances for precip on \nTues evening and again for mid to late week. The system for late \nTues is looking less and less impressive as dynamics are sheared \napart in the fast westerly flow aloft and best moisture is falling \napart as it encounters very dry air from departing high pres. Have \ntrimmed back pops from likely to chc with this forecast package. \nBetter moisture/dynamics arrive Thurs associated with pw values near \n1.5\" and some weak sfc based instability angling toward our cwa \nahead of weak cold frnt. After below normal temps on Tues, values \nshould warm back into the 70s to near 80F for Weds/Thurs, before \nslightly cooler air arrives by next Friday. Lows generally at or \nabove normal after Tuesday due to the lack of strong sfc high pres.",
